sec.p:
The cryptographic requirements are formalized by the following games, each run after system initialization and honest DA registration:
Integrity and Authenticity (Gatt−UF): The adversary receives the public parameters and public DA records, may obtain signatures from honest DAs, and may corrupt selected DAs. It wins by outputting a fresh pair (M*,ς*) that passes Algorithm 1, traces to an uncorrupted DA, and was not returned by that DA’s signing oracle.
Conditional Anonymity (Ganon): The adversary selects two uncorrupted registered DAs and a message with equal public ledger metadata. The challenger signs with one of them according to a hidden random bit. The adversary, without either signing secret, the corresponding private registration values Bi,Vi, or RR’s tracing records, wins by guessing the bit; its advantage is Pr[b′=b]−1/2.
Traceability (Gtrace): The adversary may register and corrupt DAs and obtain signatures. It wins if an accepted signature associated with an honest registration has no matching tracing record, matches more than one honest record, or is attributed to an uncorrupted DA different from its actual signer. RR’s tracing database is assumed intact.
Query Confidentiality (Gqpriv): The adversary selects two identifiers with equal public filter configuration and ledger-visible metadata and may adaptively request authorized views for other identifiers. The challenger returns the ledger view for one challenge identifier and, when an interactive processing DA is modeled, a request blinded with a fresh scalar. Neither challenge identifier may have a previously disclosed unblinded authorization token. The adversary wins by identifying the selected identifier; repeated challenge interactions use independent blinding scalars.
Algorithm 1 Traceable anonymous attestation signature verification
Input: Public parameters PP; message M; candidate anonymous attestation signature ς Output: Acceptance bit True or False 1:
Parse ς as (σ,dPK*,Y*,R*,B*,χ*), where χ*=(C*,z*), σ,Y*∈G2, dPK*,R*,B*,C*∈G1, and z*∈Zq.2:
if any group element is outside its expected group or is the identity element, or if z*∉Zq then3:
return False.4:
end if5:
Set hM←H3(M).6:
Setc*←H4(anon−r∥〈M,σ,dPK*,Y*,R*,B*,C*〉).7:
if z*Q≠C*+c*R* then8:
return False.9:
end if10:
if e^(Q,σ)≠e^(dPK*,hM) then11:
return False.12:
end if13:
Set ok1←[e^(Q,Y*)=e^(R*,MPK2)].14:
Set ok2←[e^(dPK*,P)=e^(MPK1,Y*)·e^(B*,MPK2)].15:
if ok1 and ok2 then16:
return True.17:
else18:
return False.19:
end if

The receiver DAre validates σseout, generates an acknowledgment signature σrein=σre(Λm∥info∥Nse), and returns it to DAse. It then executes Algorithm 4 on its incoming GBFPlus instance with Nadj=Nse under identical rotation rules. The core signatures σseout and σrein are preserved off-chain for independent audit and are associated with the corresponding filter sequence and ledger transaction identifier.
Algorithm 4 GBFPlus insertion
Input: (κ,η,η^,λ)-GBFPlus with successful-insertion count c<η; group-encoded data identifier Λm; long-term DA transfer/query secret key ki; RR master public-key component MPK1; assigned adjacent DA identifier Nadj∈N⊆{0,1}lDA; identifier length lDA; derived hashes Hidx,Hpay; index procedure Idxκ,η^Output: Updated GBFPlus with count c+1, or failure symbol ⊥ 1:
Initialize tag←−1.2:
u←e^(kiMPK1,Λm).3:
target←Truncλ−lDA(Hpay(u))∥Nadj and share←target.4:
(idx1,…,idxκ)←Idxκ,η^(Hidx(u)).5:
for j=1 to κ do6:
idx←idxj.7:
if GBFPlus[idx]=0 then8:
if tag=−1 then9:
tag←idx.10:
else11:
Sample ωj←${0,1}λ and set GBFPlus[idx]←1∥ωj.12:
share←share⊕ωj.13:
end if14:
else15:
Parse GBFPlus[idx]=1∥vidx and set share←share⊕vidx.16:
end if17:
end for18:
if tag=−1 then19:
return ⊥.20:
end if21:
GBFPlus[tag]←1∥share.22:
c←c+1.23:
return (GBFPlus,c).

RR queries the ledger directly, using the time window and traced DA identity to limit the search scope. To inspect a candidate sender DAse for data item m, RR computes the query token use′=e^(s1KPKse,Λm). This value matches the insertion parameter because e^(kseMPK1,Λm)=e^(s1KPKse,Λm). RR then invokes the GBFPlus lookup in Algorithm 5.
Algorithm 5 GBFPlus query
Input: (κ,η,η^,λ)-GBFPlus; query element u′∈GT; identifier length lDA; assigned identifier set N; derived hashes Hidx,Hpay; index procedure Idxκ,η^Output: Adjacent DA identifier N*, or failure symbol ⊥ 1:
Initialize payload←0λ.2:
(idx1,…,idxκ)←Idxκ,η^(Hidx(u′)).3:
for j=1 to κ do4:
idx←idxj.5:
if GBFPlus[idx]=0 then6:
return ⊥.7:
else8:
Parse GBFPlus[idx]=1∥vidx and set payload←payload⊕vidx.9:
end if10:
end for11:
Parse payload=prefix∥N*, where |N*|=lDA.12:
if prefix=Truncλ−lDA(Hpay(u′)) and N*∈N then13:
return N*.14:
else15:
return ⊥.16:
end if
